---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { groupRules } from "../../lib/group.ts";
import { impactBlurbs, impactLabels } from "../../vocabulary.ts";

const impacts = [...groupRules(await getCollection("rules"), (rule) => rule.data.impacts)];
---

<Layout title="HTML lint rules by impact" description="Every deadhead rule, grouped by what the markup costs: performance, interop, accessibility, SEO, security or upkeep.">
    <h1>Impacts</h1>
    <p class="lede">What leaving the markup in place costs. A rule can have more than one impact.</p>
    <p>
      Every rule records two separate things about its markup. Severity says how bad the
      finding is: <strong>harmful</strong> breaks something for users, <strong>deprecated</strong>{" "}
      is formally obsolete but inert, <strong>unnecessary</strong> works but is dead weight.
      Impact says who pays for it: visitors waiting on a slow load, browsers that disagree,
      people using assistive technology, search engines, the page's security, or the people who
      maintain it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two do not line up neatly. Most harmful rules cost accessibility, search, security or
      performance, because those are where markup can fail someone. Most deprecated and
      unnecessary rules cost maintenance alone: nothing behaves differently with them, but
      someone has to understand them before deleting them. A rule can carry several impacts,
      such as an <code>http-equiv="refresh"</code> that moves readers on a timer, which matters
      for accessibility, search and security at once.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each impact page explains what that cost looks like in the head and lists its rules,
      worst first. Use them to review one concern at a time, for example the{" "}
      <a href="/impacts/a11y">accessibility</a> rules in an accessibility review or the{" "}
      <a href="/impacts/seo">SEO</a> rules before a launch. The same rules are grouped by
      namespace in the <a href="/rules">rule index</a> and by feature under{" "}
      <a href="/topics">topics</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class="rules">
      {impacts.map(([impact, list]) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/impacts/${impact}`}><code>{impactLabels[impact]}</code></a> <span>{list.length} rules</span><p>{impactBlurbs[impact]}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
</Layout>
